Article 9 Protection work departments shall, based on the actual conditions of their respective industries and fields, formulate rules for the identification of critical information infrastructure and report them to the public security department of the State Council for recordation.
The following factors shall mainly be considered in formulating identification rules:
(1) the importance of network facilities, information systems, etc. to the key core businesses of the industry or field;
(2) the degree of harm that may be caused if network facilities, information systems, etc. are damaged, lose functionality, or suffer data leakage;
(3) the impact on other industries and fields by association.

Article 15 The dedicated security management department shall be specifically responsible for the security protection of the critical information infrastructure of its own unit, and perform the following duties:
(1) establish and improve network security management and evaluation and assessment systems, and draft plans for the security protection of critical information infrastructure;
(2) organize and promote the building of network security protection capabilities, and carry out network security monitoring, testing, and risk assessment;
(3) formulate emergency response plans for its own unit in accordance with the national and industry emergency response plans for cybersecurity incidents, regularly conduct emergency drills, and handle cybersecurity incidents;
(4) identify key positions in network security, organize and conduct network security work assessments, and propose recommendations for rewards and punishments;
(5) organize network security education and training;
(6) fulfill the responsibility for the protection of personal information and data security, and establish and improve personal information and data security protection systems;
(7) implement security management over services such as the design, construction, operation, and maintenance of critical information infrastructure;
(8) report cybersecurity incidents and important matters according to regulations.

Article 39 Where an operator falls under any of the following circumstances, the relevant competent department shall, according to its functions and duties, order correction and give a warning; if the operator refuses to correct or causes consequences such as endangering network security, a fine of not less than RMB 100,000 but not more than RMB 1 million shall be imposed, and a fine of not less than RMB 10,000 but not more than RMB 100,000 shall be imposed on the directly responsible person in charge:
(1) failing to promptly report the relevant circumstances to the protection work department when the critical information infrastructure undergoes significant changes that may affect its identification result;
(2) security protection measures are not planned, constructed, and used synchronously with the critical information infrastructure;
(3) failing to establish and improve cybersecurity protection systems and responsibility systems;
(4) failing to set up a dedicated security management department;
(5) failing to conduct security background checks on the head of the dedicated security management department and personnel in key positions;
(6) making decisions related to network security and informatization without the participation of personnel from the dedicated security management department;
(7) the dedicated security management department fails to perform the duties stipulated in Article 15 of these Regulations;
(8) failing to conduct a network security test and risk assessment on the critical information infrastructure at least once a year, failing to timely rectify the discovered security problems, or failing to report the relevant information as required by the protection work department;
(9) purchasing network products and services without entering into security and confidentiality agreements with the network product and service providers in accordance with the relevant provisions of the State;
(10) failing to promptly report to the protection work department when undergoing merger, division, dissolution, or other circumstances, or failing to dispose of the critical information infrastructure as required by the protection work department.
